<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Black outline around the flight-screen mini-map.

     WHY: the map sits over live video with no panel behind it, the same as the HUD readouts
     since the translucent blocks were removed. Its own tiles are pale — a road layer over a
     light background — so its EDGE disappears against a bright scene exactly as the white text
     did, and the map stops reading as a distinct instrument.

     WHY THE SAME WEIGHT AS THE TEXT: hud_text_outline_width is the one per-device value the
     whole HUD uses for its edge, so the map, the readouts and the EV slider carry a single
     treatment rather than three values that drift apart. See OutlinedTextView.

     ⚠ WIDENING THIS TO CHASE THE LAST OF THE CORNER SEAM WAS TRIED AND REJECTED. A hair of pale map can still be found
     at a corner under magnification. It is not visible in use, and one HUD edge weight is worth
     more than a perfect corner. Do not give this frame its own width.

     This is the FOREGROUND of flightMapContainer, not of the map view itself. The container is
     what toggleMapSize resizes, thus the outline follows the map to its expanded size with no
     second value to keep in step. A GradientDrawable stroke is inset by half its width inside
     the bounds, so unlike a Canvas stroke on text it cannot be clipped by the view edge.

     ⚠ THE CORNER MUST MATCH flightMapContainer'S CLIP, AND THAT CLIP IS SET IN CODE.
     This frame is the container's FOREGROUND — it draws the border but clips nothing, so a
     rounded border alone would leave square map tiles poking out past it. FlightActivity gives
     the container a rounded ViewOutlineProvider and clipToOutline.

     ⚠ THAT CLIP IS NOT THIS RADIUS. A stroke is centred on a path inset by half its width, so
     the OUTER edge this frame presents has radius (hud_pill_radius + stroke/2), which curves
     away from the corner faster than an 8dp clip does — clipping at the bare 8dp leaves a sliver
     of map outside the border. FlightActivity clips at (hud_pill_radius + stroke), putting the
     map's cut edge on the MIDDLE of this stroke so neither anti-aliased edge has to agree with
     the other to the pixel. It derives that from these same two dimens — change either one here
     and the clip follows.

     It once carried SQUARE corners, on the reasoning that a square instrument reads
     as something you look at and a rounded card reads as something you touch. The map now
     matches the pills and the toolbar instead (design decision), and the hierarchy survives it:
     hud_pill_radius is the SMALL radius, the same one every pill uses, and the large
     hud_control_radius still belongs to the toolbar capsules alone. -->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">

    <solid android:color="@android:color/transparent" />

    <corners android:radius="@dimen/hud_pill_radius" />

    <stroke
        android:width="@dimen/hud_text_outline_width"
        android:color="@color/tp_hud_outline" />
</shape>
